everyone loves a road trip right you  pack up the car set the destination and  drive across a bunch of Eastern Bloc  countries with your uncle and a bucket  of water in a car that seems to be  falling apart of the hinges part Oregon  Trail parsh mechanic simulator part  papers please this game called jalopy  really is something unique I've  certainly never played anything like it  and it's available right now on early  access for a mere twelve dollars but is  it worth all the travel are you better  off just staying at home let's find out  Jelani is a game where you nurse a four  wheeled banger across a number of  countries you stay in hotels by parts  Phillipa petrol and listen to your uncle  wax lyrical about the good old days in  the fall of socialism but it's not about  getting to your destination fast  it's about getting there in one piece  you see jalopy is a term of endearment  for a car that's let's say past its best  and yours is a like a 601 deluxe a car a  car made of parts parts that can break  parts the need to be repaired or  replaced parts that need to be taken  care of for instance the Leica has a  two-stroke engine meaning you got to  make sure there's at least some oil in  there or it's gonna run less efficiently  and get damaged more quickly but it's  not all about the mechanics there's an  element of surgeon simulator in here too  this car doesn't drive itself if you  want to pop the hood you gotta pull its  lever you're not required to change  gears or indicate with your blinkers but  you can't expect to do everything else  from turning on the ignition to flicking  on your lights and wipers using the  handbrake even rolling down the window  to hand border guards your passport  jalopy is charming as hell and all these  interactions let in spirit to a game it  doesn't say much but says what it does  rather well for instance you can buy  supplies in petrol at any gas station  you pass but the first time I did I  couldn't find my wallet to pay the lady  so I had to run back and search the  whole car of course it was in the bloody  glove compartment you've also got a  trunk full of here to help you along the  way but it works like Resident Evil's  grid system meaning it has limited space  I mean it's probably nice to have a  bucket of water so you can clean off all  the mud on your windshield but that  takes up a lot of room this inventory  management puzzle is one area where the  games early  rough edges are most apparent but in a  game center drain trying to get  something that's broken to work properly  I found myself surprisingly forgiving of  these nails though less so with some of  the behaviors of the AI drivers oh and  my uncle decided to find a faster way to  the other side of the continent saving  and quitting doesn't actually save your  progress but it will reload you back at  the last hotel you checked into which is  just as well because even in this early  version the further you get the more  challenging the gameplay becomes it all  seems rosy on your trip until you  realize you forgot to pop some oil in  the engine and it's eating itself alive  it's a very good sign for a game when  even losing is fun and even when you  find yourself on the wrong side of a bad  decision staring at a broken bridge  miles from anywhere with no fuel in the  rain while the Sun is setting you can't  help but laugh like a bangle car it's  challenging but jalopy has a wonderful  sense of charm it wears its inspiration  on its sleeves there are shades of  eightydaze surgeon sim papers please  among others but jalopy is perhaps above  anything else one of the most unique  games I've played this year if you're  interested jalopy is rough but ready to  play right now on Steam for a very  reasonable $12
 

